{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":115224,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"080f6ae79f200a7a3e6d7611b971e70e","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Blog looks fine. One issue: the mini items in sidebar appear to have narrow image columns — actually looking closely, \"Tasty Recipes\" items look reasonable. The article excerpts appear as separate gray boxes? No wait, looking at the articles, each article's excerpt looks fine.\n\nHmm, actually the article items look like the excerpt is overlapping a gray area behind... it's fine at this scale. Let me zoom one article item later if needed.\n\nMini item text slightly narrow but OK.\n\nNow 04_Blog_Post and 05_Contact. Then prototype wiring and final screenshots.\n\n04_Blog_Post structure:\n- Header (current Blog)\n- Article header: title \"Full Guide to Becoming a Professional Chef\" (centered, 44 bold), meta row (avatar Dianne Russell, name, date), intro paragraph, Photo_Blog_Chef (1280×600, rounded)\n- Article body (centered column ~800px): Q1..Q5 with answers, Photo_Cooking_Step after Q3, pull quote\n- Share: \"SHARE THIS ON:\" + social logos\n- Recommended recipes \"Check out the delicious recipes\"\n- Newsletter, Footer\n\nQ&A text from brief:\nIntro: We asked a working head chef about first jobs, long services and what it really takes to make it in a professional kitchen.\nQ1: How did you start out in the food industry? — I started washing dishes in a small family restaurant when I was sixteen. Watching the line cooks every night made me want to be on the other side of the pass, and after two years the chef finally let me prep vegetables.\nQ2: What do you like most about your job? — The rhythm of service. Hours of preparation come down to a few intense minutes, and when a full dining room goes quiet because everyone is eating, it feels like the whole team has done something together.\nQ3: Do you cook at home on your days off? — Yes, but simply. I cook the food I grew up with: rice, eggs and whatever vegetables look good at the market. Home cooking is where I remember why I love food. [Photo_Cooking_Step after third answer]\nQ4: What would your advice be to young people looking to get their foot in the door? — Start anywhere and be the person who shows up early. Ask questions, taste everything and write down what you learn.\nPull quote: \"Technique can be taught; curiosity and reliability can't.\"\nQ5: What is the biggest misconception that people have about being a professional chef? — That it is all creativity. Most of the job is consistency: making the same dish perfectly two hundred times a night, keeping the kitchen clean and looking after your team.\n\nLayout: photo after Q3's answer. Pull quote after Q4 (order in brief: Q3 answer [photo], Q4, pull quote, Q5). Brief lists \"a pull quote and Photo_Cooking_Step after the third answer\". DATA order: Q3 ... [Photo] Q4 ... Pull quote ... Q5. So quote sits between Q4 and Q5. I'll place: Q3 + photo, Q4, pull quote, Q5.","signature":""}],"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb6a87e1-77d2-4750-aa78-08940da487c1","uuid":"88e6463a-1933-4962-9e93-3abe5419f3e7","timestamp":"2026-11-01T20:21:11.199Z","thinking_duration_ms":12144}
